  "text": "In October of 2014 we converged on one subject. Nineteen of us said \"in west africa\"; seventeen said \"the ebola virus.\" Sixteen of us each said \"to the united states,\" \"for disease control,\" \"the ebola outbreak,\" and \"spread of ebola\" — the peaks landing on October 16 and October 17, within a day of one another. Fourteen said \"centers for disease control,\" and fourteen said \"the spread of ebola.\"\n\nWe were reading from the same page. The phrase \"in west africa\" first appeared in our record on 2013-05-03; \"the ebola outbreak\" not until 2014-08-01. By mid-October the language had gathered nineteen of us onto a single line.\n\nWe report this without comment: on those two days we spoke of one virus, in one region, arriving in one country, and we did so in nearly the same words.",
